  "body": "A plain bar meshed as two rings now wraps in every orientation. The cause was the axis fit leaning by rounding, not a feature on the seam. The seam refusal now names its real causes.\n\n### What was wrong\n\n- A bar meshed as two rings (long side facets, axis-fan caps; this is how CAD exports a plain cylinder) was refused by the rotary wrap: \"a feature that is not turned (a flat, a keyway, a cross hole) crosses the rotary wrap's seam\". On `main` 0015347, with the issue's fixture (R6, Z ±40, 2 mm tool, Ø13 stock), n 16 at turn 0, n 64 at turn 0 and n 64 at turn 0.3 are refused (15, 37 and 38 seam columns). Only n 16 at turn 0.3 wraps.\n- The cause is the axis fit, as the issue guessed, though the size differs: the lean is tens of microradians, not 1e-9.\n  - On a two-ring bar, every side facet is a long right triangle. Its circumcenter (where `axis.rs` anchors its normal line) is the midpoint of the long diagonal, which is the bar's mid-station. No normal line can see a tilt about that point.\n  - The eigen direction came out exactly Z (residual 7.4e-14 mm). The Gauss-Newton refinement (`axis_refine::refine_dir`) then returned a tilt of 2.6e-5 rad at n 16 and 2.5e-6 rad at n 64, and the fit kept it because its residual was \"lower\" by rounding noise (7.4159e-14 vs 7.4196e-14). At turn 0.3 the noise fell the other way, so the same bar fit exactly and wrapped.\n  - With the axis leaning, the cap centres sat about 1 µm off it at arbitrary azimuths. Every cap facet near the ±π branch then read as a seam straddler, and the seam check counted far more than two columns.\n\n### What changed\n\n- `axis_refine::worth_refining`: the fit does not refine an eigen line whose residual is already below `CONCURRENT = 1e-9` mm per mm of mean radius. That line meets every normal line to rounding, and a refinement can only add a tilt the rows can't see. A real lean (the #62 uneven cone, microns of miss) is far above that floor and is still refined.\n- `rotary_seam::SEAM_TH_TOL = 1 / WELD` (1e-6 rad, was 1e-9): seam vertices within the weld grid's grain, read as an angle, share a column. That is the same relative quantum the column profiles are compared at, and coarser than a float32 STL's azimuth noise. A flat's ragged vertices stand whole degrees apart; every D-flat refusal test still refuses.\n- The seam refusal also names a mesh whose diameters were tessellated with different column counts, and says what to do about each cause (\"rotate a feature clear of the seam, or export the model with one column count\"). The phrase \"crosses the rotary wrap's seam\" is kept.\n- Decision record: `docs/decisions/228-seam-columns.md`. Widening only the seam tolerances was dropped: with the axis 2 µm off at the bar's end, the column profiles also differed by 2 µm in radius.\n\n### Tests (`crates/motion/tests/rotary_dflat.rs`)\n\n- `a_two_ring_bar_fits_its_true_axis`: n 16 and 64, at turns 0 and 0.3. The fitted axis leans less than 1e-12 rad.\n- `a_plain_two_ring_bar_wraps_in_every_orientation`: the same four bars wrap, with more than 50 cuts and no cut tip inside the true polygon cross-section (0.05 margin).\n- `a_shaft_meshed_with_mixed_column_counts_is_refused_naming_the_tessellation`: a stepped shaft with 16 columns on the neck and 64 on the body is refused, and the message names the column counts. Wrapping it would also answer the issue, but nothing pins what such a wrap would cut, so the refusal is what this test pins.\n- With `axis.rs`, `axis_refine.rs` and `rotary_seam.rs` put back to `main`, all three fail; the four existing D-flat tests pass either way.\n\n### What ran (Windows 10, local, built from upstream `main` 0015347 plus this commit)\n\n- `scripts/gate.ps1 -Tier batch` (every debug suite minus the four slow binaries, the ui crate, and clippy over every crate): 2400 tests pass. The axis fit is shared by the rotary kinds, the turning frame and the lathe spindle law, so this is the run that matters. It includes `lathe_spindle_fit`, which pins the #62 uneven-tessellation lean that the refinement exists for.\n- The only failures are the three UI pixel snapshots (`egui_shell_snapshot`, `egui_shell_snapshot_light`, `tool_editor_snapshot`). They fail on clean `main` on this machine by the same pixel counts (1544, 1815, 2080; #255).\n- Clippy (`-D warnings`, all targets): clean.\n- Not run: the release tier's slow binaries (`motion:finish3d`, `motion:pivot5d`, `motion:flow`, `app:center_cut_multiaxis`), Linux, macOS. I did not drive the app through `camcraft-cli mcp` for this one; the motion and app suites above are the check.\n\nChangelog: The rotary wrap no longer refuses a plain bar meshed as two rings: the axis fit stops leaning it by rounding, and the seam refusal names a mixed tessellation as well as a feature (#228)\n\nFixes #228\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
